  "appVersion": "0.9.0",
  "description": "Two shared staff serve four counter transactions (checkout, check-in, purchase, repair drop-off) plus a final pickup hand-over. Hire is a single entity (HireJob) moved through stages via RELEASE: checkout (draws one bike atomically from a 15-bike inventory container, BikesAvailable, and balks if the pool is empty), then an Out On Hire stage (an unlimited-in-practice dummy resource, OnHire, used purely to model the rental duration), then check-in (fills BikesAvailable back). Return is no longer an independent arrival stream — every check-in corresponds to an actual earlier hire. A repair job is a separate single entity moved through stages via RELEASE: drop-off, then the Repair Queue for the actual repair. Once repaired, the shop notifies the customer and the job waits in a Travel Queue for a short return delay (another dummy resource, TimeAway) before reaching the Pickup Queue for the final hand-over. The repair job is protected (taskPriority=2) throughout, except while actual repair work is in progress (taskPriority=1), during which it is preemptable: any waiting checkout/check-in/purchase/drop-off/pickup customer interrupts an in-progress repair once both staff are busy, and the interrupted repair re-queues and resumes later."
